{
  "id": "gezaixi_taiwanese_opera",
  "name": "Gezaixi (Taiwanese opera)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Hokkien-language opera that took shape in the Yilan area of Taiwan in the early twentieth century from gezai song and local folk theatre, spread to southern Fujian and the Minnan diaspora, and is on China's national intangible heritage list (2006); it was recorded on 78s in the 1930s and later on radio and television. Its core accompaniment is the 'four pillars' of kezaixian coconut fiddle, daguangxian, yueqin and bamboo flute (the generic bamboo-flute card stands in for it), with gongs and cymbals for stage action, and its arias rest on the seven-character tune and the weeping tunes.",
  "recipe": "classic mid-1930s gezaixi, art Music, east Asian branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. coconut shell bright piercing ensemble lead kezaixian, wutong paulownia sustained daguangxian, yueqin, bamboo credited bamboo flute unspecified, opera bending gong tamtam, naobo. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
